// @test: typecheck-fail
// @level: L0
// @tier: 0
// @tags: modules, name-resolution, module-path, totality
// @timeout: 60000
// @expected-error: E100
// @description: A call through a module path whose ROOT names nothing is
//   an unbound name — not a fresh type variable that satisfies anything.
//
//   Everything around this shape was already checked, which is why it
//   survived. Measured in one batch with a positive control:
//
//       a.b.f()             real path                     compiles
//       a.b.absent()        real module, no leaf          E100
//       a.zzz.f()           real module, no middle        E100
//       zz.absent()         2 segments, unknown root      E100
//       zz.a.b.c.absent()   5 segments, unknown root      E100
//       p.sub.deep.absent() rooted in a VALUE             E404
//
//       zz.nope.absent()    3 segments, unknown root      SILENT -> nil
//
//   One shape, and only because it fell between two gates: short enough
//   that the longer-path route did not judge it, unknown enough that the
//   module route declined. The chain handler then minted a fresh type
//   variable for the receiver — a root with no value binding is assumed
//   to be a module — so the call satisfied ANY declared return type and
//   evaluated to `nil`.
//
//   `module_member_is_missing` answers "is this a module MISSING a
//   member" and answers only on positive evidence, which is right: saying
//   yes about a chain it cannot judge would reject working code. Its
//   mirror, `module_root_is_unknown`, answers the case it declines.
//
//   Task: T0806.

module a {
    public module b {
        public fn f() -> Bool { true }
    }
}

fn main() {
    // The control lives in the sibling spec; this file must FAIL, and a
    // file that also contains a passing call would still fail for the
    // wrong reason if the gate regressed to rejecting everything.
    zz.nope.absent();
}
